    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The complement component 1, q subcomponent-like 3 (C1ql3) gene is expressed in neurons in important brain regions such as the hippocampus, the basolateral amygdala and the insular cortex, which are known to regulate spatial memory, emotional memory, and interoception, respectively.  Interoception is the brain’s awareness of bodily states, and it is important for mammals to feel the rewarding effects of a drug like cocaine.  Preliminary cocaine conditioned place preference experiments on C1ql3 mutant mice showed that C1ql3 may have a role in drug reward behavior.  Based on the literature and preliminary data, we hypothesized that C1QL3 has a role in regulating the interoception necessary for such drug-associated conditioning.  In order to test our hypothesis, adult mice with conditional alleles for C1ql3 were intracranially injected with either control viruses or viruses expressing Cre recombinase to knock out C1ql3 expression in the insular cortex.  Incidentally, the virus also diffused into the amygdala, removing C1ql3 expression in a region known as the basolateral amygdala.  After recovery, several behavior assays were performed on the mice to understand the nature of the cocaine CPP phenotype.  The virus-injected conditional mutant mice showed normal drug-associated conditioning, yet they exhibited emotional memory deficits, suggesting that our original hypothesis needed refinement.  A thorough behavioral characterization of the mutant mice demonstrated that, rather than specifically affecting drug-associated conditioning through alterations in interoception, C1QL3 has a more general role in regulating short-term emotional memory, which is most likely due to its expression in the amygdala.  The lack of CPP phenotype after virus targeting to the insular cortex is likely due to the only partial inactivation of the basolateral amygdala. Further research is required to definitively demonstrate that C1QL3 expressed in the basolateral amygdala is required for mice to form memory associations between contexts and emotional salient stimuli.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
